//? Title: Native plotting gallery
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

> Plotting expansion plan

The existing API covers scatter and line plots, categorical bars, histograms,
box plots, pie charts, normal Q-Q plots, function curves, heatmaps, image grids,
and scatterplot matrices. GGPlot adds data-frame mappings, faceting, density,
smoothing, ribbons and areas. RLayout composes independent panels.

- Add named RPlot layers for ordered data and uncertainty: steps, stems,
  lollipops, areas, ribbons, and capped error bars.
- Add horizontal, floating, grouped and diverging stacked bars, plus waterfall
  charts for cumulative changes.
- Extend distribution plotting with empirical CDFs, rugs, kernel density and
  violins. Reuse the Gaussian density estimator already used by GGPlot.
- Add bubble plots, vector arrows and scalar contours. Reuse the native
  marching-squares algorithm on rectangular grids.
- Exercise all chart families through complete, editable C++ programs and the
  shared renderer. Check geometry, invalid inputs, and exported panels.

Remaining gaps include equal-aspect and polar coordinates, transformed axes,
interactive 3D surfaces, geographic projections and streaming dashboards.
These need coordinate or rendering support beyond adding chart layers.

\toc

> Chart inventory and implementation order

This inventory covers the major chart families and names representative
variants. A compound chart can combine existing layers even when it has no
dedicated constructor. The first batch adds candlestick, OHLC, dumbbell,
stacked-dot and population-pyramid layers. The following batches add
statistical diagnostics, part-to-whole charts, multivariate coordinates and
projected height fields. The inventory marks the remaining work by family.

| Family | Methods available or composition shown here | Additional chart types to implement |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Relationships | Scatter, colored scatter, bubbles, line, function curve, segments, scatterplot matrix, dumbbell, Bland–Altman | Jittered scatter, connected scatter, hexbin, rectangular bin counts, bivariate density, residual-versus-fit, scale–location, leverage, influence, added-variable and coefficient plots |
| Ordered data | Line, step, area, stem, lollipop, waterfall, stacked area, ACF and PACF | Indexed series, percent stacked area, streamgraph, horizon, sparkline, control chart, run chart, calendar heatmap, cycle plot, lag plot, seasonal subseries |
| Categories | Vertical bars, horizontal bars, grouped bars, diverging stacks, floating bars, schedule, pie, population pyramid, Pareto | Cleveland dot plot, slopegraph, bump chart, percent stacked bars, diverging Likert, mosaic, spine, Marimekko, waffle, funnel, bullet, gauge, donut and radial bars |
| Distributions | Histogram, Gaussian density, rug, violin, box, normal Q-Q, empirical CDF, stacked dots, frequency polygon, two-sample Q-Q | Weighted histogram, cumulative histogram, jittered strip, beeswarm, boxen, raincloud, ridgeline, stem-and-leaf, P-P, normal probability and return-level plots |
| Intervals and studies | Ribbons, capped errors, interval bars, forest, Kaplan–Meier | Caterpillar, fan, interval dot, prediction calibration, reliability, ROC, precision–recall, lift, gains, decision curve, cumulative incidence and cumulative hazard |
| Finance | Candlestick, OHLC, waterfall | Volume panels, drawdown, equity curve, Heikin–Ashi, Renko, Kagi, point-and-figure, depth and order-book imbalance |
| Matrices and fields | Heatmap, raster, label grid, contours, vectors | Clustered heatmap, correlogram, confusion matrix, correlogram glyphs, Hinton matrix, spectrogram, filled contours, streamlines, wind barbs and vector magnitude maps |
| Radial and directional | Pie, normalized radar | Polar scatter and line, rose histogram, wind rose, polar heatmap, radial dendrogram and circular timeline |
| Hierarchy and flows | Node and edge diagram primitives, area-proportional treemap | Sunburst, icicle, dendrogram, packed circles, Sankey, alluvial, parallel sets and chord diagram |
| Multivariate and sets | Scatterplot matrix, facets, parallel coordinates, ternary | Andrews curves, biplot, glyph matrix, Venn, Euler, UpSet and dissimilarity ordination |
| Networks | Arc diagrams, node and edge primitives | Force-directed, layered, circular, bipartite, adjacency matrix and edge-bundled networks |
| Geography | Choropleth with projected rings | Proportional symbols, dot density, cartogram, flow map, geographic hexbin, route, isochrone and gridded map |
| Three-dimensional | Oblique height-field wireframe | 3D scatter and line, surface, mesh, 3D bars, isosurface, slice plot, volume render and terrain |

Implement direct 2D layers first. Add reusable statistical transforms for
diagnostics, survival and density before writing their renderers. Then add
coordinate transforms and equal aspect for radial and geographic charts, layout
algorithms for networks and flows, and camera/depth support for 3D charts.
Prioritize geometry and data tests before renderer snapshots for each batch.

> Relationships, sequences and uncertainty

Use points for paired observations, curves for a sampled function, steps for
values held until the next time, and stems for discrete impulses. Areas fill
to a baseline. Ribbons fill between two curves, and error bars show intervals
at individual observations. Disconnected segments and text can annotate any
of these plots.

```{cpp, file="figures/plot_gallery_relationships.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/datamunge.hpp>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>

struct Sine : datamunge::Callback {
    double call(double x) override { return std::sin(x); }
};

int main() {
    using datamunge::Plot;
    using datamunge::graphics::RLayout;
    const std::vector<double> x{0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
    const std::vector<double> y{0.8, 1.4, 1.1, 2.2, 2.6, 2.3};
    Sine sine;
    auto figure = RLayout::create(4, 2).size(1120, 1200);
    figure.add(Plot().points_colored(x, y, {{37, 99, 235}, {234, 88, 12}})
                    .abline(.8, .35, {100, 116, 139}).title("Scatter and reference line"))
          .add(Plot::curve(sine, 0, 6.28).abline_h(0).title("Sampled function"))
          .add(Plot().steps(x, y).title("Step series"))
          .add(Plot().stems(x, {0.8, -1.4, 1.1, 2.2, -2.6, 2.3}).title("Impulse series"))
          .add(Plot().area(x, y).lines(x, y).title("Filled area"))
          .add(Plot().ribbon(x, {0.4, .9, .7, 1.7, 2.1, 1.9}, {1.2, 1.9, 1.5, 2.7, 3.1, 2.7})
                    .lines(x, y).title("Uncertainty ribbon"))
          .add(Plot().error_bars(x, {0.5, 1, .9, 1.9, 2, 2}, {1.2, 1.9, 1.7, 2.5, 3, 2.8})
                    .points(x, y).title("Capped intervals"))
          .add(Plot().segments({0, 1}, {0, 1}, {1, 2}, {1, 0})
                    .text(1, 1.2, "Join").ylim(-.1, 1.4).title("Segments and annotation"));
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    figure.fit_panels().save_svg("build/plot_gallery_relationships.svg");
}
```
// result_begin:
// \image(figures/plot_gallery_relationships.svg)
// result_end

> Categories, composition and changes

Grouped bars compare series side by side. Stacked bars accumulate positive
and negative terms on opposite sides of zero. A waterfall displays consecutive
changes in a running total. Floating rectangles can also represent time ranges
in a schedule.

```{cpp, file="figures/plot_gallery_categories.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/datamunge.hpp>
#include <filesystem>

int main() {
    using datamunge::Plot;
    using datamunge::graphics::RLayout;
    const std::vector<double> x{0, 1, 2, 3};
    const std::vector<double> counts{6, 3, 8, 5};
    const std::vector<std::string> labels{"North", "East", "South", "West"};
    const std::vector<std::string> seasons{"Spring", "Autumn"};
    auto figure = RLayout::create(4, 2).size(1120, 1200);
    figure.add(Plot::barplot(counts, labels).title("Vertical bars"))
          .add(Plot().horizontal_bars(x, {6, -3, 8, 5}).y_tick_labels(labels)
                    .title("Horizontal bars"))
          .add(Plot().grouped_bars(x, {counts, {4, 7, 5, 6}}, seasons)
                    .x_tick_labels(labels).title("Grouped bars"))
          .add(Plot().stacked_bars(x, {{3, -2, 5, -4}, {-2, 4, 2, -1}}, seasons)
                    .x_tick_labels(labels).title("Diverging stacks"))
          .add(Plot().waterfall(x, {8, -3, 5, -2})
                    .x_tick_labels({"Start", "Cost", "Sales", "Fees"}).title("Waterfall"))
          .add(Plot().lollipop(x, counts).x_tick_labels(labels).title("Lollipop"))
          .add(Plot().rectangles({0, 1, 3}, {-.3, .7, 1.7}, {2, 4, 5}, {.3, 1.3, 2.3})
                    .y_tick_labels({"Design", "Build", "Review"}).x_label("Week").title("Schedule"));
    figure.fit_panels().add(Plot::pie({53, 29, 18}, {"Solar", "Wind", "Hydro"})
                                .size(360, 360).title("Shares of a total"));
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    figure.save_svg("build/plot_gallery_categories.svg");
}
```
